{"about":"66df759e09c49faedd7f1b9e7ebf1d8ceeac2fed60fcd2cbf4ca3d2592289264","body":"By asserting we choose a theory because it is our 'best current guess at being closer to the truth,' the conjecture smuggles in a probabilistic or inductive assumption under a different name. To claim one theory is a 'better guess' than another regarding future performance or structural reality implies a judgment about the likelihood of its truth based on past success, which is precisely the inductive inference Popper sought to eliminate. If corroboration reports only past performance without implying future reliability, calling it a 'better guess' is semantically empty or logically inconsistent.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The pragmatic contradiction in 'best current guess'"}